burn_ym2151: save the render position and carried samples in savestates

BurnYM2151Render() carries nYM2151Position, nFractionalPosition and the last few samples in
pBuffer from one frame into the next, and BurnYM2151Scan() saved none of them. A loaded state
then rendered as many samples as the history before the load left over. Drivers whose
YM2151 IRQ is timed by rendered samples rather than BurnTimer (Battle Garegga) ran their Z80
differently after a load than in the run that saved the state, so recordings desynced in
the sound CPU's RAM hundreds of frames later (docs/performance.md, "FBNeo and savestates").

Upstream: not merged yet; drop this file when the pinned commit contains the change
(docs/cores.md, "Patches").

diff --git a/src/burn/snd/burn_ym2151.cpp b/src/burn/snd/burn_ym2151.cpp
index 1b5c22a..cbc734f 100644
--- a/src/burn/snd/burn_ym2151.cpp
+++ b/src/burn/snd/burn_ym2151.cpp
@@ -373,6 +373,20 @@ void BurnYM2151Scan(INT32 nAction, INT32 *pnMin)
 	}
 
 	SCAN_VAR(nBurnCurrentYM2151Register);
+	// The render position and the samples BurnYM2151Render() carries into the next frame.
+	// Without them a loaded state renders as many samples as the history before the load
+	// left over, and drivers that time the YM2151's IRQ by rendered samples (no BurnTimer)
+	// run their sound CPU differently after a load than in the run that saved the state.
+	SCAN_VAR(nYM2151Position);
+	SCAN_VAR(nFractionalPosition);
+	{
+		struct BurnArea ba;
+		memset(&ba, 0, sizeof(ba));
+		ba.Data = pBuffer;
+		ba.nLen = 4 * 4096 * sizeof(INT16);
+		ba.szName = "YM2151 carried samples";
+		BurnAcb(&ba);
+	}
 
 	BurnYM2151Scan_int(nAction); // Scan the YM2151's internal registers
 
